"1. Vaccine. 2025 Aug 30;62:127441. doi: 10.1016/j.vaccine.2025.127441. Epub 2025 \nJul 4.\n\nLessons learnt during establishment of COVID-19 active vaccine safety \nsurveillance in nine African countries.\n\nCutland CL(1), Gutu K(2), Yun JA(3), Izu A(4), Mahtab S(5), Peter J(6), Ansah \nNA(7), Obaro S(8), Tilahun B(9), Jambo K(10), Sow S(11), Kagucia EW(12), \nChicumbe S(13), Dlamini T(14), Browne M(15), Clothier H(16), Griffin J(17), \nJiang Y(18), Lee A(19), Ghebreab L(20), Madhi SA(21), Black SB(22); Active \nvaccine safety surveillance team.\n\nCollaborators: Ndlovu X(23), Thwala BN(24), Mpungose A(24), Ansah PO(25), Hama \nA(25), Igumbor E(26), Ebruke B(27), Toni A(28), Mumba D(29), Haidara FC(30), \nSang S(31), Sigilai A(31), Khosa C(32), Sevene E(32).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Wits African Leadership in Vaccinology Expertise (Wits-Alive), School of \nPathology, Faculty of Health Science, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa. Electronic address: clare.cutland@wits.ac.za.\n(2)South African Medical Research Council Vaccines and Infectious Diseases \nAnalytics Research Unit, University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South \nAfrica. Electronic address: kimberley.gutu@wits.ac.za.\n(3)Wits African Leadership in Vaccinology Expertise (Wits-Alive), School of \nPathology, Faculty of Health Science, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa. Electronic address: jessica.yun@wits.ac.za.\n(4)Wits African Leadership in Vaccinology Expertise (Wits-Alive), School of \nPathology, Faculty of Health Science, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa; South African Medical Research Council Vaccines and \nInfectious Diseases Analytics Research Unit, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa. Electronic address: alane.izu@wits-vida.org.\n(5)South African Medical Research Council Vaccines and Infectious Diseases \nAnalytics Research Unit, University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South \nAfrica. Electronic address: sana.mahtab@wits-vida.org.\n(6)Allergy and Immunology Unit, University of Cape Town Lung Institute; Division \nof Allergology and Clinical Immunology, Groote Schuur Hospital, University of \nCape Town. Electronic address: Jonny.Peter@uct.ac.za.\n(7)Navrongo Health Research Centre, Navrongo, Ghana. Electronic address: \nnana.ansah@navrongo-hrc.org.\n(8)International Foundation Against Infectious Disease (IFAIN), Abuja, Nigeria. \nElectronic address: stephenson255@gmail.com.\n(9)Center for Digital Health and Implementation Science, University of Gondar, \nEthiopia. Electronic address: binigcms@gmail.com.\n(10)Malawi-Liverpool-Wellcome Trust Clinical Research Programme, Malawi. \nElectronic address: kjambo@mlw.mw.\n(11)Centre for Vaccine Development (CVD), Mali, Bamako. Electronic address: \nssow@cvd-mali.org.\n(12)KEMRI-Wellcome Trust Research Programme, Kilifi, Kenya. Electronic address: \nEKagucia@kemri-wellcome.org.\n(13)Instituto Nacional de Sa\u00fade, Mozambique. Electronic address: \nsergio.chicumbe@ins.gov.mz.\n(14)National Immunization Programme, Eswatini.\n(15)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \nm.browne@auckland.ac.nz.\n(16)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \nhazel.clothier@mcri.edu.au.\n(17)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \njennifer.griffin@auckland.ac.nz.\n(18)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \ny.jiang@auckland.ac.nz.\n(19)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \narier.lee@auckland.ac.nz.\n(20)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \nluam.ghebreab@auckland.ac.nz.\n(21)Wits African Leadership in Vaccinology Expertise (Wits-Alive), School of \nPathology, Faculty of Health Science, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa; South African Medical Research Council Vaccines and \nInfectious Diseases Analytics Research Unit, University of the Witwatersrand, \nJohannesburg, South Africa. Electronic address: shabir.madhi@wits.ac.za.\n(22)Global Vaccine Data Network, Auckland, New Zealand. Electronic address: \nstevblack@gmail.com.\n(23)South African Medical Research Council Vaccines and Infectious Diseases \nAnalytics Research Unit, University of the Witwatersrand, Johannesburg, South \nAfrica.\n(24)Allergy and Immunology Unit, University of Cape Town Lung Institute.\n(25)Navrongo Health Research Centre, Navrongo, Ghana.\n(26)International Foundation Against Infectious Disease (IFAIN), Abuja, Nigeria; \nSchool of Health Systems and Public Health, University of Pretoria, Pretoria, \nSouth Africa; Centre for Infectious Disease Research, Nigerian Institute of \nMedical Research, Lagos, Nigeria.\n(27)International Foundation Against Infectious Disease (IFAIN), Abuja, Nigeria.\n(28)Center for Digital Health and Implementation Science, University of Gondar, \nEthiopia.\n(29)Malawi-Liverpool-Wellcome Trust Clinical Research Programme, Malawi.\n(30)Centre for Vaccine Development (CVD), Mali, Bamako.\n(31)KEMRI-Wellcome Trust Research Programme, Kilifi, Kenya.\n(32)Instituto Nacional de Sa\u00fade, Mozambique.\n\nBACKGROUND: Globally, several gaps in vaccine safety surveillance exist, \nparticularly in low- and middle- income countries (LMICs). Establishing and \nmaintaining vaccine surveillance platforms in resource-constrained settings \nposes significant challenges. These countries often rely on paper-based medical \nrecords and immunization cards, lack unique patient identifiers across the \nhealthcare systems, have limited electronic data capture capabilities, and face \na shortage of clinical reviewers for case assessments. This report highlights \nthe establishment of two active vaccine safety surveillance studies across nine \nAfrican countries: (i) Active COVID-19 vaccine safety surveillance (ACVaSS) in \neight COVAX 92 Advanced Market Commitment (AMC-92) eligible African countries \nincluding Ethiopia, Ghana, Kenya, Mali, Malawi, Mozambique, Nigeria and Eswatini \nand (ii) the South African COVID-19 vaccine safety surveillance study (SA-CVSS).\nMETHODS: Both ACVaSS and SA-CVSS were hospital-based sentinel active \nsurveillance studies designed to monitor the safety of COVID-19 vaccines in the \naforementioned COVAX AMC-92 countries and South Africa, a middle-income African \ncountry. Patients presenting to healthcare facilities with illnesses resembling \npre-selected adverse events of special interest (AESIs), were enrolled, with \ninformed consent, into the studies. The Brighton Collaboration Case Definitions \nwere applied to classify AESIs.\nFINDINGS: Over 60,000 admitted patients were screened and over 12,700 eligible \npatients were enrolled in 18\u00a0months. Despite challenges in accessing and \nabstracting data from predominantly paper-based medical and vaccination records, \nthe identification of specific AESIs and estimating association with vaccination \nstatus was feasible in LMIC healthcare facilities.\nCONCLUSIONS: The establishment of active vaccine safety surveillance sentinel \nsites is achievable in LMICs, though the lack of digital medical records \nhindered data accessibility and availability. Regulatory authorities, health \ndepartments and organizations supporting immunization programs must prioritize \nthe development, maintenance and funding of active vaccine safety surveillance \nsystems. Such surveillance is crucial to ensuring that new vaccines are properly \nmonitored and assessed for safety following their introduction and use in these \npopulations.\nFUNDING: The SA-CVSS study was funded by a US CDC Grant to the GVDN (grant \nreference: CDC Funder Award Number: 1 NU38CK000485-01-00), the South African \nMedical Research Council (SAMRC) and the Task Force for Global Health \n(RVD_CDC-COV). Gavi, The Vaccine Alliance, funded the ACVaSS study (Agreement \nreference: MEL10500921).\n\nCopyright